Implement a full Web IDE experience inside MIM Expert and replace the current Git ChangeSet page with it.

First inspect the existing Angular/Node architecture, repo selector, ChangeSet model, Git actions and Kiro integration. Reuse existing functionality; do not create duplicate Git/session systems.

1. Web IDE
Integrate a browser-based VS Code experience (prefer code-server/OpenVSCode rather than building an editor from scratch).

The currently selected repo from the global repo selector must open as the IDE workspace and switch automatically when the selected repo changes.

Provide normal IDE capabilities:
- file explorer
- editor
- search
- multi-file tabs
- source control
- changed-files view
- side-by-side Git diff/review
- branch/status/history
- terminal where supported
- existing user-triggered Build/Test/Commit/Push actions.

Goal: users should be able to perform normal development/review work without opening a separate local IDE.

2. Preserve ChangeSets
Remove the need for a separate ChangeSet page, but DO NOT remove the ChangeSet backend concept.

Changes implemented by MIM Expert must remain linked to their ChangeSet/chat/fix metadata.

When such changes exist, the Web IDE Source Control/changed-files view must expose them and allow the user to review exact diffs.

Existing rule remains:
MIM Expert implements code → STOP.
Build/Test/Commit/Push remain explicit USER actions.

3. Quick Edit Assistant
Add a small floating “Ask”/AI icon inside the Web IDE.

Clicking it opens a compact chat drawer called Quick Edit.

Quick Edit must use the existing Kiro CLI / mim-expert runtime in QUICK_EDIT mode; do not create another user-facing agent.

Automatically provide context:
- currently selected/open repo
- workspace path
- current branch
- active file
- selected code/text when available
- current local Git changes.

4. Quick Edit behavior
The user should be able to give a direct instruction such as:
“Make this null-safe”
“Add tests for this method”
“Change this API to return 404 when not found”
“Refactor this implementation”

Then automatically:
understand repo context → inspect required code → determine required files → implement the complete change → save files → refresh IDE/Source Control → show changed files/diffs → STOP.

Do NOT create a spec/plan/RCA first.
Do NOT ask for confirmation.
Do NOT ask follow-up questions when the change can reasonably be inferred from code/context.

If the request is genuinely unsafe or impossible to determine unambiguously, make NO changes and return the exact blocker.

5. Strict scope
Quick Edit may modify ONLY the repository currently open in the Web IDE.

It must never silently modify another service/repo.
Any cross-service change must go through the normal main MIM Expert analysis/fix flow.

6. IDE synchronization
After Quick Edit or normal MIM Expert modifies files:
- refresh affected files and Source Control immediately
- preserve unrelated existing local changes
- never overwrite/discard user modifications
- show exact resulting diffs.

Repo switching must also update both the IDE workspace and Quick Edit context.

7. Security/behavior
Do not expose credentials/tokens to the IDE/chat.
Do not automatically build, test, commit, push, pull or publish code from Quick Edit.
Preserve all existing Git safeguards and current MIM Expert functionality.

Target experience:

Main MIM Expert → analysis/RCA/multi-service fixes
Web IDE → complete browser development + Git review
Web IDE Ask → one-prompt direct code modification of the currently selected repo only

Implement this end-to-end and integrate it cleanly into the existing application.



